{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794555662,"nonce":"VWQJCIH81LurikKzo0i9HTwn5rM=","title":"A flat list scrolls in time proportional to its rows in a real window, which the harness cannot see","message":"Found by running scroll_bench in a window (release, macOS, 480x800 at 2x) with BLINC_MOTION_TRACE and RUST_LOG=blinc_app::frame_timing=trace. The scroll benchmark in blinc_recorder, which the scroll-as-rows work (3b364ef) was measured on, shows none of it.\n\nLIST=flat, rows on the CPU (the default). Every scrolled frame takes the fast path (cache_blit), and the frame cost grows with the rows:\n- 1,000 rows: median 3.7 ms. 5,000 rows: 14.4 ms. 20,000 rows: 57 ms, p99 94 ms, a frame every 85 ms.\n- BLINC_NODE_TABLE=1 at 20,000 rows: 35 ms.\n- Nearly all of it is the GPU side of the frame: gpu_us 15.5 ms of a 15.9 ms frame at 5,000 rows, with the same figure without the trace.\n- The renderer logs \"Primitive count N exceeds buffer capacity 20000, truncating\" on every frame: N is 58,390 at 5,000 rows and 246,890 at 20,000, about 12 a row. The cached batch holds every row, not the rows in view, and is dispatched each frame. At 20,000 rows it is cut to the first 20,000 primitives.\n- BLINC_MOTION_PROMOTION=1 (the baked way) at 20,000 rows aborts on the first frame: a wgpu validation error, a write of 23.7 MB into a 4.8 MB buffer.\n\nWhat the harness does differently. scroll_perf paints the same list culled: 0.3 to 0.5 ms a frame at 5,000 rows with the example's own tree (a header, then a full-height scroll), at pixel density 1 and 2, with or without a settle before the first paint. So it is not the layout, the density, the settle or the trace. Two differences are left. Headless::paint_timed does not wait for the GPU, so a GPU-side cost never shows in it. And the window's first paint is a build, a layout and a paint in one frame, while the harness lays out first.\n\nCause not found. In the harness the walker culls against the scroll container (viewport_cull_scrolls); in the window the batch it caches holds every row. To check first: whether the scroll node is in viewport_cull_scrolls in the window's tree, which overscan record_scroll_row_node records for it (an infinite one paints everything), and the primitive count of the batch after the first paint. A harness test that waits for the GPU and reads that count would hold it.\n\nFor comparison, the virtual list (scroll_bench's default), 100,000 rows: no capacity warnings, no gap over 40 ms, but every scrolled frame is a full paint (0 of 1,051 fast) at median 2.8 ms, p99 10.6 ms, because rows mount as it scrolls and the layout changed since the rows were painted (37795b1e). That is the cost of a virtual list on rows, not this fault.\n\nRelated: 3b364ef, 04efbde, 122c606.","files":null}]}